
Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields Utilities list;

register(Utilities);
define
    mlBag; mlNum;
    mlField code={
        mlNum = 7;
        cerr "ML-1 CONTROL  +/ a FIELD attaches":;
        mlBag +/ mlNum;
        mlLen := mlBag.listLengtH;
        cerr "ML-1 length = " *mlLen " (want 1)":;
        };
    mlLiteral code={
        cerr "ML-2 SUBJECT  +/ a LITERAL must refuse":;
        mlBag +/ 7;
        cerr "ML-2 DID NOT REFUSE -- a literal was attached as a member":;
        };
    mlAfter code={
        mlLen := mlBag.listLengtH;
        cerr "ML-3 length AFTER the refusal = " *mlLen " (want 1 -- nothing was attached)":;
        };
    ;
mlField(mlBag);
mlLiteral(mlBag);
mlAfter(mlBag);
cerr "MEMBERLIT SENTINEL":;
stop();

memberLitT -- +/ REFUSES A LITERAL RIGHT SIDE, BY PRESENCE.

Membership attaches a NODE to a list. A literal is a value with no node to attach, so
`bag +/ 7` is asking the membership channel to do the value channel's job. The test is
`isLiteral`, which the parse already knows -- presence, not kind. A classifier-based gate
was the withdrawn S3, and a gate that waits for one is a gate that never lands.

ML-1 IS THE ANTI-VACUITY CONTROL and is not decoration: the same operator with a FIELD on
the right must still attach, or ML-2's refusal is equally consistent with +/ being broken.
ML-3 reads the length in a LATER action, because a refusal is terminal for the action and
the line after the refused statement does not run -- so the only way to see that nothing
was attached is from outside.

⚠ WHY THIS IS ITS OWN FILE RATHER THAN A testPrecedence ROW. The row was written there
first and took the fixture to exit 138 with ZERO bytes on both streams. The signal is
SIGBUS, not SEGV.

⚠ AND THE FIRST READING OF IT, PUBLISHED IN 89002da, WAS WRONG. It said 14 and 15
characters parse, 16 and 17 die, and 20 parses again -- so a window rather than a
threshold. The 20 came from a row with a DIFFERENT BODY, so the two runs differed by more
than the name. Re-swept with the body held fixed, lengths 8 through 32: everything at 15
and below passes, everything at 16 and above dies, monotone. No window.

WHAT IS SOLID, measured with controls:
  - It is FILE SIZE and not the tag. One padding byte in a COMMENT takes the same file
    from passing to dying, and so does padding in the DEAD REGION AFTER stop(), which is
    never parsed. So the fault is in the READ, not the parse.
  - Two unrelated fixtures -- testPrecedence and this file, padded only after its stop()
    -- have the IDENTICAL threshold: 32265 bytes passes, 32266 dies.

WHAT IS NOT SOLID, said out loud rather than tidied away: a synthetic file with no
include, padded as one enormous line, passes at 32266, dies at 40000, and passes again at
60000. That is not monotone, so a single global size constant does NOT explain it and
line structure or content position is doing something. The probe was stopped there rather
than pushed, because the next step is reading the input-stack read itself and that is its
own stroke.

DO NOT cite the 32265 number as a general limit. Cite it as: two real fixtures, same
preamble, same threshold.

⚠ THE STACK IS NOT IT -- MEASURED 2026-09-11, and this closes the standing guess rather
than leaving it open. A file padded to 32266 bytes dies identically at every stack size
offered: 8176 KB (the default), 16384, 32768 and 65520. The ulimit was confirmed applied
inside the subshell before each run, and under the LARGEST stack the threshold is still
